+An ID is unique among entities of the same type within a session, not across all entity types, so the same string can identify one entity of each type. User messages, Agent messages, Agent thoughts, and session-directed messages are separate types even though they share the `messageId` field. For example, an Agent may report a thought and a response derived from the same model output with the same `messageId`; they remain two distinct entities.
 The Agent **MUST NOT** reuse an ID for a different entity of the same type within a session. Clients **MUST** key entities by both type and ID. An update applies only to the entity of its own type: an `agent_thought` whose `messageId` matches an existing Agent message creates or updates a thought and leaves the message unchanged. Likewise, a field that references an entity identifies an entity of one specific type; for example, the `messageId` returned by `session/prompt` identifies a user message.
 An entity's first update fixes its timeline position in receive order: it follows previously received timeline content and precedes content first received later. Later updates and chunks patch the entity in place without moving it, including after the session reports `idle`. Updates for different entities may interleave, and a new ID does not end any other entity. Clients that show an entity outside the timeline, such as a plan panel, may ignore its position.
+### Session-directed messages (unstable)
+The Agent may report outgoing and incoming messages between sessions, separately from user-facing responses and tool calls. The outer `params.sessionId` identifies the transcript being updated. Every `session_message` or `session_message_chunk` requires `messageId`, scoped to that transcript. Optional `senderSessionId` and `recipientSessionId` on `session_message` identify the participants; chunks carry only content, as `agent_message_chunk` does. Include them on the first `session_message` when available, before the first chunk of a streamed message; later updates may omit them or add missing identities.
+For example, an incoming message in the child can name its participants, then stream its content:
+```json
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "method": "session/update",
  "params": {
    "sessionId": "child_session",
    "update": {
      "sessionUpdate": "session_message",
      "messageId": "received_message_1",
      "senderSessionId": "parent_session",
      "recipientSessionId": "child_session"
    }
  }
}
```
+```json
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "method": "session/update",
  "params": {
    "sessionId": "child_session",
    "update": {
      "sessionUpdate": "session_message_chunk",
      "messageId": "received_message_1",
      "content": { "type": "text", "text": "Please investigate " }
    }
  }
}
```
+Chunks append normal message content for the same IDs. A `session_message` upsert may replace the whole `content` array or patch message metadata by top-level key, as described in [Metadata Patches](/protocol/v2/extensibility#metadata-patches). Omission leaves either field unchanged; `null` clears it. `content: []` also clears accumulated content. Chunk `_meta` is scoped to that chunk and does not patch message metadata.
+Clients can render "To …" with a recipient link or "From …" with a sender link. Without sufficient participant metadata, use a generic inter-session presentation and add links when the metadata arrives. Omitted or `null` participant IDs mean not supplied and leave previously known identities intact. The two views have independent message IDs. Optional `senderMessageId` on the incoming view and `recipientMessageId` on the outgoing view name the other view's `messageId`, so a Client can go from either view to the other. They follow the participant-ID rules and can arrive in a later `session_message`; Clients must not link views by equal IDs alone. The Agent reports each side only when observed; Clients must not synthesize an incoming entry from an outgoing one or forward the content themselves. Neither view acknowledges completed recipient processing.
